# JavaScript DOM Traversal

**DOM Traversal** means moving through the HTML document using JavaScript.

Instead of selecting an element directly with an ID or class, DOM traversal allows us to move:

* From a child to its parent
* From a parent to its children
* Between sibling elements
* From an element to the nearest matching ancestor

This is useful when working with:

* Dynamic HTML
* Buttons and forms
* Lists
* Cards
* Tables
* Event handling
* Interactive web pages

---

# What is the DOM?

DOM stands for **Document Object Model**.

When the browser loads HTML, it converts it into a tree-like structure.

For example:

```html
<div>
    <h2>Hello</h2>

    <p>Welcome</p>

    <button>Click</button>
</div>
```

The browser represents it approximately like this:

```text
        div
      /  |  \
    h2   p  button
```

Each HTML element becomes a **node**.

JavaScript can move between these nodes.

That is DOM traversal.

---

# 1. Selecting an Element First

Before traversing the DOM, we usually need an element to start from.

```html
<div id="card">
    <h2>Product</h2>
    <p>₹500</p>
</div>
```

JavaScript:

```javascript
const card = document.getElementById("card");
```

Now `card` becomes our starting point.

---

# 2. `parentElement`

`parentElement` moves **up** one level.

```html
<div id="card">
    <button id="buy">Buy Now</button>
</div>
```

```javascript
const button = document.getElementById("buy");

console.log(button.parentElement);
```

The result is:

```html
<div id="card">
    <button id="buy">Buy Now</button>
</div>
```

Think:

```text
button
   ↑
parent
```

---

# 3. `children`

`children` gives us the element's direct child elements.

```html
<div id="card">

    <h2>Product</h2>
    <p>₹500</p>
    <button>Buy</button>

</div>
```

```javascript
const card = document.getElementById("card");

console.log(card.children);
```

The children are:

```text
h2
p
button
```

Important:

`children` returns **element nodes**, not text nodes.

---

# 4. Accessing a Specific Child

Because `children` behaves like a collection, we can access elements using indexes.

```javascript
console.log(card.children[0]);
```

Returns:

```html
<h2>Product</h2>
```

And:

```javascript
console.log(card.children[1]);
```

Returns:

```html
<p>₹500</p>
```

Indexes start from `0`.

```text
children[0] → first child
children[1] → second child
children[2] → third child
```

---

# 5. `firstElementChild`

Instead of using:

```javascript
card.children[0];
```

we can use:

```javascript
card.firstElementChild;
```

Example:

```html
<div id="card">
    <h2>Product</h2>
    <p>₹500</p>
</div>
```

```javascript
console.log(card.firstElementChild);
```

Output:

```html
<h2>Product</h2>
```

---

# 6. `lastElementChild`

Similarly:

```javascript
card.lastElementChild;
```

returns the last child element.

Example:

```html
<div id="card">
    <h2>Product</h2>
    <p>₹500</p>
    <button>Buy</button>
</div>
```

```javascript
console.log(card.lastElementChild);
```

Output:

```html
<button>Buy</button>
```

---

# 7. Sibling Elements

A sibling is an element that shares the same parent.

Example:

```html
<div>

    <h2>Product</h2>

    <p>₹500</p>

    <button>Buy</button>

</div>
```

The siblings are:

```text
h2
p
button
```

They all have the same parent:

```text
        div
      /  |  \
    h2   p  button
```

---

# 8. `nextElementSibling`

`nextElementSibling` moves to the next sibling.

```javascript
const heading = document.querySelector("h2");

console.log(heading.nextElementSibling);
```

Output:

```html
<p>₹500</p>
```

The movement is:

```text
h2
 ↓
p
 ↓
button
```

---

# 9. `previousElementSibling`

`previousElementSibling` moves backwards.

```javascript
const button = document.querySelector("button");

console.log(button.previousElementSibling);
```

Output:

```html
<p>₹500</p>
```

Movement:

```text
button
   ↑
   p
   ↑
  h2
```

---

# 10. `closest()`

`closest()` is one of the most useful DOM traversal methods.

It searches upward through the element's ancestors until it finds a matching element.

Example:

```html
<div class="card">

    <div class="content">

        <button class="buy">
            Buy
        </button>

    </div>

</div>
```

JavaScript:

```javascript
const button = document.querySelector(".buy");

const card = button.closest(".card");

console.log(card);
```

The browser moves upward:

```text
button
  ↑
content
  ↑
card
```

It finds:

```html
<div class="card">
```

---

# Why `closest()` Is Useful

Suppose we have many cards:

```html
<div class="card">
    <h2>Phone</h2>
    <button class="delete">Delete</button>
</div>

<div class="card">
    <h2>Laptop</h2>
    <button class="delete">Delete</button>
</div>
```

We can find the card containing the clicked button:

```javascript
document.addEventListener("click", function(event) {

    if (event.target.classList.contains("delete")) {

        const card = event.target.closest(".card");

        card.remove();
    }

});
```

We don't need to give every card a unique ID.

---

# 11. `parentElement` vs `closest()`

These are different.

### `parentElement`

Moves exactly **one level up**.

```javascript
element.parentElement;
```

### `closest()`

Can move multiple levels upward until it finds a match.

```javascript
element.closest(".card");
```

Example:

```text
card
 ↓
content
 ↓
button
```

From `button`:

```javascript
button.parentElement
```

gives:

```text
content
```

But:

```javascript
button.closest(".card")
```

gives:

```text
card
```

---

# 12. `children` vs `childNodes`

This difference is important.

Consider:

```html
<div>
    <h2>Hello</h2>
    <p>World</p>
</div>
```

### `children`

Returns element children:

```javascript
element.children;
```

Result:

```text
h2
p
```

### `childNodes`

Returns all types of nodes, including text nodes.

```javascript
element.childNodes;
```

Because whitespace and line breaks can also be represented as text nodes, the result may contain more nodes than `children`.

For most basic DOM traversal, `children` is easier to work with.

---

# 13. Traversing a List

Consider:

```html
<ul id="users">

    <li>Nikitha</li>
    <li>Rahul</li>
    <li>Ananya</li>

</ul>
```

We can get the list:

```javascript
const users = document.getElementById("users");
```

Then:

```javascript
console.log(users.children);
```

We can loop through them:

```javascript
for (const user of users.children) {

    console.log(user.textContent);

}
```

Output:

```text
Nikitha
Rahul
Ananya
```

---

# 14. Changing an Element Through Traversal

DOM traversal isn't only for reading elements.

We can also modify them.

```html
<div id="card">

    <h2>Old Title</h2>

    <p>Product description</p>

</div>
```

JavaScript:

```javascript
const card = document.getElementById("card");

card.firstElementChild.textContent = "New Title";
```

The HTML becomes:

```html
<h2>New Title</h2>
```

---

# 15. Traversing Up and Down

We can combine different traversal properties.

```javascript
const button = document.querySelector("button");

const card = button.closest(".card");

const title = card.firstElementChild;

console.log(title.textContent);
```

The path is:

```text
button
   ↑
.card
   ↓
first child
   ↓
title
```

This is extremely useful when working with repeated UI components.

