# Common DOM Traversal Properties

| Property / Method        | What it does                        |
| ------------------------ | ----------------------------------- |
| `parentElement`          | Gets the parent element             |
| `children`               | Gets direct child elements          |
| `firstElementChild`      | Gets the first child element        |
| `lastElementChild`       | Gets the last child element         |
| `nextElementSibling`     | Gets the next sibling               |
| `previousElementSibling` | Gets the previous sibling           |
| `closest()`              | Finds the nearest matching ancestor |
| `childNodes`             | Gets all child nodes                |

---

# DOM Traversal Mental Model

Think of the DOM as a family tree:

```text
                 Parent
                /      \
           Child 1    Child 2
                         |
                      Grandchild
```

### Move upward

```javascript
element.parentElement;
```

### Move downward

```javascript
element.children;
```

### Move sideways

```javascript
element.nextElementSibling;
element.previousElementSibling;
```

### Find a specific ancestor

```javascript
element.closest(".class");
```

---

# Key Takeaways

* DOM traversal means moving between elements in the DOM.
* `parentElement` moves upward one level.
* `children` moves downward to direct child elements.
* `firstElementChild` gets the first child.
* `lastElementChild` gets the last child.
* `nextElementSibling` moves to the next sibling.
* `previousElementSibling` moves to the previous sibling.
* `closest()` searches upward for the nearest matching ancestor.
* `children` returns elements, while `childNodes` includes other node types such as text nodes.
* DOM traversal becomes especially useful when working with repeated or dynamically generated elements.

